```

Here, `*desc = ring->desc[idx]` is a struct copy from shared memory into a local `struct xdp_desc` — so desc is snapshotted once, and validation is done on the snapshotted copy. That is the "validate = use" pattern... but wait. The struct assignment copies the whole struct at once. Technically the compiler could copy it in multiple loads, but each field is copied once into the local desc. Then validation uses `desc->addr`, `desc->len`, `desc->options` from the local copy. Callers use the same local copy. So this is fine — the validated copy is the used copy. Not a double fetch. Although the struct copy itself isn't READ_ONCE, each field is read once into the local. There could be a theoretical issue if the compiler reloads, but practically the copy is a single memcpy. This is the same as upstream. Not a variant.

Similarly `xskq_cons_read_desc_batch()` — `descs[nb_entries] = ring->desc[idx];` then parse_desc on the local copy. Fine.

